{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Miriam spoke.</b> It was she who spoke; Aaron either agreed or was silent. Therefore he was punished." "The meaning of <b>and she spoke [with]</b> — the bet is in a pejorative sense, as in 'the people spoke against Elohim.' It is also found in a praiseworthy sense, and even in the context of prophecy. Some say that Moses was king over Cush and took a Cushite woman. The translator [Onkelos] rendered it 'beautiful,' in an honorific sense, just as the Ishmaelites call white pitch by the same term, and we too call a blind person 'one abundant in light.' Now it is impossible that a term of praise should be turned into a pejorative. Others say that Cush the Benjamite is Saul, as in 'like the Cushites.' I have already explained this. But it seems right to me that this Cushite woman is Zipporah, for she is a Midianite, and the Midianites are Ishmaelites, and they dwell in tents, as it is written, 'the curtains of the land of Midian.' Because of the sun's heat they have no whiteness at all, and Zipporah was dark, resembling a Cushite woman." "The meaning of <b>for he had taken a Cushite woman.</b> This is the statement that Miriam made. How honorable are the words of our ancestors, who said of the elders: fortunate are their wives, woe to their wives! For they suspected that Moses refrained from lying with Zipporah only because she was not beautiful."], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:17:25.963814032Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}